{"name":"Moon Rock","hex":"#897d76","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moon rock","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Rock from the surface of the moon, but especially any geological specimen brought back to Earth by the Apollo program.","A mixture of crack cocaine and heroin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon_rock"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moon rock","blocks":[{"text":"Moon rock or lunar rock is rock originating from Earth's Moon. This includes lunar material collected during the course of human exploration of the Moon, and rock that has been ejected naturally from the Moon's surface and landed on Earth as lunar meteorites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Lunar_Olivine_Basalt_15555_from_Apollo_15_in_National_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg/330px-Lunar_Olivine_Basalt_15555_from_Apollo_15_in_National_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon_rock","wikidata":"Q318438"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon","wikidata":"Q405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Lunar_Sample_Processing_Facility_NASA_JSC_DSCN0202.JPG/500px-Lunar_Sample_Processing_Facility_NASA_JSC_DSCN0202.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Lunar_Sample_Processing_Facility_NASA_JSC_DSCN0202.JPG/1280px-Lunar_Sample_Processing_Facility_NASA_JSC_DSCN0202.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Lunar_Sample_Processing_Facility_NASA_JSC_DSCN0202.JPG/500px-Lunar_Sample_Processing_Facility_NASA_JSC_DSCN0202.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Processing facility in Lunar Sample Building at JSC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALunar_Sample_Processing_Facility_NASA_JSC_DSCN0202.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/MoonRockAirandSpaceMuseum.JPG/500px-MoonRockAirandSpaceMuseum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/MoonRockAirandSpaceMuseum.JPG/1280px-MoonRockAirandSpaceMuseum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/MoonRockAirandSpaceMuseum.JPG/500px-MoonRockAirandSpaceMuseum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Slice of Moon rock at the National Air and Space Museum in Washington, DC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoonRockAirandSpaceMuseum.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Apollo_15_Genesis_Rock.jpg/500px-Apollo_15_Genesis_Rock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Apollo_15_Genesis_Rock.jpg/500px-Apollo_15_Genesis_Rock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca